From 4318695fda1a23e03564b19d2011fbb4d89c8148 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 13:31:56 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] CUDA: do not group same-activation matvecs on this
 device by default

The grouped mmvq launch ported in this series is a large loss on GB10, which
is the opposite of its effect on RDNA4, so this turns it off by default and
keeps it behind an opt-in.

Measured with llama-bench (Laguna-XS-2.1-Q4_K_M, -ngl 99, tg128, sm_121,
CUDA 13.0), interleaved with stock in the same session:

  stock                        96.50 / 96.39 / 96.66 / 96.68 tok/s
  series with grouping ON      82.06 / 82.44 tok/s          (-15%)
  series with grouping OFF     99.17 / 98.97 tok/s          (+2.5%)

The cause is not the CUDA-graph path: stock with GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_GRAPHS=1
measures 94.75 tok/s, so graph replay is worth only ~2% on this box and cannot
account for a 15% swing. The grouped kernel is simply slower here than the
per-op mul_mat_vec_q instantiations it replaces - on sm_121 those are
specialised (own launch bounds, own fusion variant) in a way the segment-routed
grouped kernel is not, and the dispatches it saves are cheap on this device.
Every other launch-count patch in the series (rms_norm, rope, set_rows
grouping, and the quantize folds) stays on and is part of the +2.5%.

The implementation is left in place, correct and byte-exact, behind
GGML_CUDA_ENABLE_MMVQ_GROUP=1 - nothing groups unless that is set - so the
lever stays available for CUDA devices where it does pay.

Output is unaffected: greedy text and full float top-5 logprob hashes match
the stock build on 3 prompts with the default (grouping off) build.
---
 ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu | 13 ++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
index 5fd801f..fb3cadb 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
@@ -4942,9 +4942,16 @@ static void ggml_cuda_graph_evaluate_and_capture(ggml_backend_cuda_context * cud
                                                    std::atoi(getenv("GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_PRE_ADD_NORM"));
             static const bool disable_norm_quant = getenv("GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_NORM_QUANT") != nullptr &&
                                                    std::atoi(getenv("GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_NORM_QUANT"));
-            static const bool disable_mmvq_group = getenv("GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MMVQ_GROUP") != nullptr &&
-                                                   std::atoi(getenv("GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MMVQ_GROUP"));
-            const bool mmvq_group_enabled = !disable_mmvq_group && stream_ctx.concurrent_events.empty();
+            // Measured on GB10 (sm_121, CUDA 13.0, Laguna-XS-2.1-Q4_K_M, llama-bench
+            // tg128): grouping these matvecs costs 15% of decode throughput
+            // (96.5 -> 82.1 tok/s) where it gains on RDNA4. This build path keeps
+            // CUDA graphs enabled, so the dispatches the grouping removes are graph
+            // nodes that cost almost nothing to replay, while the grouped kernel
+            // gives up the per-op specialised instantiation. It is therefore OFF by
+            // default here and kept behind an opt-in for devices where it does pay.
+            static const bool enable_mmvq_group = getenv("GGML_CUDA_ENABLE_MMVQ_GROUP") != nullptr &&
+                                                  std::atoi(getenv("GGML_CUDA_ENABLE_MMVQ_GROUP"));
+            const bool mmvq_group_enabled = enable_mmvq_group && stream_ctx.concurrent_events.empty();
             static const bool log_mmvq_group_stats = getenv("GGML_MMVQ_GROUP_STATS") != nullptr;
             int64_t mmvq_group_stats_buf[2] = {0, 0};
             int64_t * mmvq_group_stats = log_mmvq_group_stats ? mmvq_group_stats_buf : nullptr;
